and one of the things to remember is  that all of the passengers on that duck  boat were from somewhere else they were  visitors but Branson is a town where in  some ways there really are no strangers  we saw that over the last two days from  local residents who tried to save the  duck boat passengers to the families who  came here in mourning the mayor of  Branson may have actually said it best  in the hours after the accident quote  were a very resilient community we  bounce back and we take care of each  other whether we know you or whether we  don't know you  Russell McKay drove more than 200 miles  from st. Louis when he got the news  about his friends I was working in the  garage come on let's go we're going down  here I said I gotta see my Rosie I'll  never see her again there's an  outpouring of grief in Branson from  friends of the victims like McKay to  locals some of whom tried to prevent the  tragedy from being worse Pat Cox owns  the State Park marina on Table Rock Lake  he called his manager Roger carpenter  just after 6 p.m. Thursday with orders  to evacuate the marina so when you heard  them that there were duck boats that  were still out on the water what did you  think that time I got a call from Mitch  our general manager said hey I just got  a call from the Showboat Branson Belle  there's a duck out there capsized get  out there he didn't even get all the  words out of his mouth and I was going  with boats how many of you went out  there three boats right away cell phone  video captured the duck boat fighting to  stay afloat in the treacherous waters  what did you expect to see when you got  out there I expect the depot in  lifejackets joking around you're gonna  go grab him out of the water as fast as  we could what did you  when you got there and all we seen was  empty life jackets in empty life rings  and I mean your your heart just drops  carpenter did save two men who had gone  out to rescue victims only to become  stranded themselves they also picked up  three of the deceased you've had 24  hours or so almost to think about what  you saw and what happened what's gone  through your mind in that time it's  shame it's crying shame  there'll be plenty of experts that study  this but I think an ounce of prevention  on the front end would have prevented  most of this from happening we've heard  that from a lot of people actually  around here Pat Cox has owned the marina  that he has there the store for almost  30 years and he said he'd never seen a  tragedy like this on the lake this isn't  the only place obviously where duck  boats are a popular tourist attraction  you can find them from Seattle to Boston  just how safe are they though we're  gonna take a closer look a little bit  later for now we want to go back to  Anthony and Michelle in New York so many  questions about those life jackets Gina  and and and the gesture of leaving the  flowers on the cars that you showed us  is incredibly moving yeah what struck me  the most is again they didn't know these  people there were a few people out here  who knew some of the victims but these  were total strangers I talked with one  woman they're tourists here they didn't  know anything but they heard about what  happened and she said we just we had to  come here it was just something that we  felt we had to do we were so moved and  they were talking to people they never  knew that they said they'll stay in  touch with now forever they feel bonded  together because they all experienced it  in some way they take away certainly  know where those life jackets are grab  them be ready to grab them when at any  moment Dana Jacobson in Branson Missouri  this morning thank you so much Dana
 

